{"name":"Starfruit","hex":"#e4d183","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"starfruit","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of star fruit."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/starfruit"}],"entries":[{"title":"Starfruit (album)","blocks":[{"text":"starfruit is the fourth studio album from the American pop group stephaniesǐd."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6b/Starfruit_%28album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Starfruit_(album)","wikidata":"Q65081396"},{"title":"Carambola","blocks":[{"text":"Carambola, also known as star fruit, is the fruit of Averrhoa carambola, a species of tree native to tropical Southeast Asia. The edible fruit has distinctive ridges running down its sides. When cut in cross-section, it resembles a star, giving it the name of star fruit. The entire fruit is edible, usually raw, and may be cooked or made into relishes, preserves, garnish, and juices. It is commonly consumed in Southeast Asia, South Asia, the South Pacific, Micronesia, parts of East Asia, the United States, parts of Latin America, and the Caribbean. The tree is cultivated throughout tropical areas of the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Averrhoa_carambola_ARS_k5735-7.jpg/330px-Averrhoa_carambola_ARS_k5735-7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Carambola","wikidata":"Q181560"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Carambola_1z_.jpg/500px-Carambola_1z_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Carambola_1z_.jpg/1280px-Carambola_1z_.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/